 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+## Limiting the number of scrape targets per job
+
+A misconfigured service discovery source (for example, an overly broad cloud API query or catalog filter that got
+accidentally removed) may suddenly return a huge number of targets for a single `job_name`. Scraping all of them
+at once can spike `vmagent` CPU and memory usage and degrade scraping for every other job running on the same
+`vmagent` instance.
+
+The `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag sets a default limit on the number of scrape targets,
+which can be simultaneously active for a single `job_name`. The limit applies to the combined set of targets
+produced by all the service discovery mechanisms (and `static_configs`) configured for that `job_name` — it isn't
+tied to any particular discovery backend. By default this limit is disabled (i.e. set to `0`), so existing setups
+aren't affected.
+
+The limit can be overridden on a per-job basis via the `max_scrape_targets` option at `scrape_config`:
+
+```yaml
+scrape_configs:
+- job_name: my-job
+  max_scrape_targets: 1000
+  kubernetes_sd_configs:
+  - role: pod
+```
+
+When the number of discovered targets for a `job_name` exceeds the configured limit, `vmagent` keeps scraping
+only the allowed number of targets (chosen deterministically by sorting on target URL, so the selection stays
+stable across service discovery refreshes), while the rest are excluded from scraping instead of being scraped
+all at once or causing the whole job to be rejected.
+
+Excluded targets are shown as `DROPPED (too many targets)` at the `/service-discovery` page, together with the
+regular per-job active/total target counts, and are included in the `droppedTargets` list at the `/api/v1/targets`
+page. Additionally, the `vm_promscrape_scrape_pool_targets{scrape_job="<job_name>", status="excluded"}` metric
+exposes, for every affected `job_name`, how many discovered targets are currently being left out because of the
+limit — see [these docs](#monitoring) for other targets-related metrics exposed by `vmagent`.
+
 ## High availability
 
 It is possible to run multiple **identically configured** `vmagent` instances or `vmagent`
